+### The reader by the parse's tiles
+
+The parse hands each tile to the stage after it as the tile is done
+(`ParseTileSink`), and the reader takes its first passes there and its
+expressions' walk by the same tiles
+([the record](https://github.com/buster14a/buster-performance/tree/d5c242144a03078d91bff31219cbee1c5ebaeb13/docs/measurements/all-core-tiles-2536-2026-11-23)'s
+"The reader by the parse's tiles"; `--reader-tiles=off` is the reader as
+one, the twin).
+
+- **The sink** (`CheckInputPrepass`). As each tile's tree is final, the
+  reader's passes whose inputs are the parse's alone run on it: the tree's
+  traversal (`parse_tree_columns_range`: the postorder, the positions, the
+  ends, the children's lists and the subtrees' sizes), the counts with the
+  walk's syntax bits and marks (`check_input_count_range`), and the
+  declarations' prefixes (`check_input_named_prefixes_range`). A pass that
+  reads a token's successor stays that far behind the tile's end, whose next
+  tokens the parse hasn't written. The columns are the unit's, on the scratch
+  where the reader had them, and the reader takes them for its own; a unit
+  parsed as one gets them in one call.
+- **The tiles' ends** are the parse's, listed by the sink: runs of whole
+  top-level declarations. (The tree's roots are not: an old-style
+  definition's parameter declarations are roots of their own, and the reader's
+  walk at the declarator writes at the body's brace. A check of what a tile's
+  walk writes ahead found it.) A parse that refuses a tile gives the unit as
+  one tile, and the reader then runs as one.
+- **The expressions' walk** takes the tiles one at a time: each tile's classes
+  (`check_input_expression_classes_range`), then its coded walk, which reads
+  them and the columns they read while they are in the cache. The graph is
+  the unit's, appended tile after tile, so its nodes are numbered as before.
+
+Paired in one hold, three passes each, the same executable with the
+reader's parts switched (`t6-pair`):
+
+| `sqlite3.c` | the parser's tiles alone | with the reader's |
+| --- | ---: | ---: |
+| eight workers, MB/s | 342.6 | 354.0 |
+| their user cycles | 909.9 M | 870.2 M |
+| their kernel cycles | 220.9 M | 211.3 M |
+| the parse, ms alone → at eight | 4.8 → 8.8 | 7.4 → 15.7 |
+| `check-input`, the same | 42.5 → 95.5 | 39.7 → 80.1 |
+| one worker, wall ms | 114.31 | 114.06 |
+| its peak resident set | 238.2 MB | 238.2 MB |
+
+- **+3.3% at eight workers**, 40 M user cycles a compile; one worker takes the
+  same time and memory.
+- **The traversal is most of it**: derived from a tile's parents while they are
+  in the cache, it is +2.3% and +3.9% at eight workers in two holds. In the
+  parser's own arena it cost a compile alone 1.4% (15 MB more at its peak); on
+  the scratch it costs nothing.
+- **The expressions' walk by tiles** is 11 M user cycles of the 40 M.
+- **The counts and the prefixes** alone are within the passes' spread.
+- **The walk's columns cleared a tile ahead of it** (27 bytes a token of
+  clears that the walk is the first to write) measured within the spread too,
+  356.3 against 354.0 MB/s at eight workers and 114.40 against 114.06 ms alone,
+  and were taken out again: the clears are streams the cache's prefetch
+  serves, alone and beside others.
+- **The same bytes**: `sqlite3.c`'s object by tiles of 1, 64 and 4,096 tokens,
+  and the objects, diagnostics and statuses of c-testsuite's 220 programs and
+  the repository's 1,578 test programs by tiles of a declaration and of 16
+  tokens, are the unit's as one.
+
+What `check-input` still waits for at eight workers is its walk and the
+rounds after it, each a pass over the unit, and 34,000 base-page faults,
+which the kernel's part below takes.
+
